WEBVTT

00:00:35.290 --> 00:00:51.303
Good. All right. Thanks everyone for joining for today's events. The topic is all the books, which is nicely unspecific and specific at the same time. So my name is Marcus.

00:00:52.635 --> 00:01:13.121
I started this group and I do events every 2 weeks and provide them now also in podcast form for those who cannot always be there. And the topic is about Jung and his life and all the books that he has written because they are very closely associated with each other.

00:01:13.748 --> 00:01:37.966
So the first book I'm going to show to you is his autobiography, which is a pretty great way to start. Uh, it's called Memories, Dreams, Reflections, and it's a kind of autobiography. He has not directly written it. Himself, but he had help from a young lady who basically talked to him and she wrote it down. And I think he did some additional editing.

00:01:38.465 --> 00:01:55.901
So he has written it pretty late in life, which means there's a huge focus of his later part in life, especially as he traveled more there. He traveled to India and he traveled to Africa in his 50s and his 60s and so on. Those were all very important experiences for him.

00:01:56.383 --> 00:02:19.119
So he talks about a lot his early life as a child because there were a lot of experiences for him, especially in dream form that were, let's, let's say, giving direction to his life and already started certain themes that would go on for him over his whole life. It's very good. It gets recommended a lot.

00:02:19.328 --> 00:02:33.660
So there's like 3 books that always get recommended, and this one is very high to get into Jung because he already explains a lot of concepts and how he came about. And understanding his life helps in understanding his work.

00:02:35.074 --> 00:02:52.354
And the biggest part of his work that's available, that is the Collected Works, which is a collection of 20, 21 books. Depending on how you count them. And those are all works that were directly written by him.

00:02:52.564 --> 00:03:11.823
So it's books, essays, introductions, letters, books of different sizes, big books and small books. And this was planned roughly in the, I think, '40s, '50s. Starting in America.

00:03:11.903 --> 00:03:36.693
So he basically organized his texts in English first and then started to have German and Italian translations done in accordance to that. So he has written most of his stuff in German because he was Swiss, but he's also written a lot and give a lot of seminars in English. So this is one big part, and this is Collected Works that are existing already now for a long time.

00:03:37.446 --> 00:04:01.492
And a couple of years ago, over 10 years ago, there was a Philemon Foundation, or still is a Philemon Foundation, that wanted to publish all the works that are not included in the Collected Works because there is a lot of stuff, especially seminars, which he has not really written down himself, but he had students taking notes. And out of these notes, they created the books.

00:04:01.668 --> 00:04:24.227
And the seminars are very, very, very good. And I will get into that more when I show you the book, especially when you're interested in Dream Analysis. When you read the Collected Works, there's very thin information on that. So the most information about dreams and dream analysis comes from his seminars on these topics.

00:04:25.436 --> 00:04:40.627
So you have the Collected Works, which are in a kind of chronological order. When you see the books, they are starting with his earliest works, but later the chronology gets a little bit mixed up.

00:04:41.236 --> 00:05:02.999
And his ideal approach and his wish for readers when they want to experience Jung was really go through the work in pure chronological form. This means the way he has written it, to see his different stages and his different conclusions that are building on top of each other. A lot of Jung is building.

00:05:03.970 --> 00:05:21.850
So he has three very big blocks in his life, and I mentioned this a lot because it's important. The first block is very psychoanalytic, where he's working as a scientist, as a practitioner, as professor, and creates his friendship with Freud that then breaks down.

00:05:22.262 --> 00:05:43.955
Leads to his second part of life, which is the collective unconscious with the anima and the animus and the shadow and all of these concepts. And then it goes over into alchemy, which is an incredibly big block. And you will see all the books on alchemy. So I will start with the Collected Works.

00:05:44.485 --> 00:05:58.605
I will start with the very first one and It's called, I have to look up because I have some in German. This one is in German because the German books are often cheaper. And it's called Psychiatric Studies.

00:05:59.585 --> 00:06:24.125
And this is really the earliest part of his life as his creative writing life, where he is a student in Basel and writes his PhD thesis to become a doctor and to become a practitioner. And it's very interesting in the sense that you have already there all those building blocks and themes he is interested in. He has a niece who is a medium.

00:06:24.785 --> 00:06:40.789
So she was used in séances to give spirit talks or talking in different tongues and speaking in the voices of dead people and so on. And he really observed this.

00:06:41.150 --> 00:07:03.279
He was studying psychology and he tried to understand what was happening because it was a huge craze back then to have a medium and to ask questions and so on. And his niece was very proficient in that in the sense she would completely change her character, her voice, and all this. This he studied and wrote his PhD thesis about.

00:07:04.130 --> 00:07:26.815
And there are already those breadcrumbs of the unconscious. And autonomous psychic forces and this connection also through mythology and also the occult, as Jung was incredibly open for everything. Freud was narrow in his mind and religion, occult, everything was humbug.

00:07:27.924 --> 00:07:52.731
But Jung really saw in everything that was a human product in any way or form, doesn't matter. An indication how the psyche works. And this he used for his investigations. So this is pure psychoanalytic written articles and academic papers where he's building his career because he has a career.

00:07:52.795 --> 00:08:10.148
He has an academic career. He studies, he gets his PhD, he becomes also a professor, starts to lecture. He works as a psychiatrist in Burghölzli, which was a well-known clinic for people with mental illnesses.

00:08:11.505 --> 00:08:34.464
This he all uses to build and expand his knowledge. And this was the first book. This is now the second book, which is called Experimental Researches. And here he already starts to develop his own methods. Psychology back then is pretty much the Wild West.

00:08:34.705 --> 00:08:53.183
It's a new field, relatively new. So everybody involved, you can get to know and talk to, and everybody is still alive. And he also tries to get up with his own methods because nobody really knows how to measure things, how to theorize things, and so on.

00:08:53.867 --> 00:09:12.637
And he creates this device called the association test, which is giving people a word and get their reaction. And he would measure the time very accurately. He would have a very accurate watch and write it down how long a person takes to give a reaction word.

00:09:13.472 --> 00:09:30.332
And he has a list of 200 words, and by giving this list, and collecting the reaction times, he would find out that certain words would take longer to give an answer to. And he would have a standardized list so he could compare now people.

00:09:30.813 --> 00:09:52.978
And he would find out that this reaction time was not an accident, but rather it was a topic that was hot in the unconscious of the person. So they became distracted. The word back then was a topic. To have a complex, which is something like a little gremlin in your mind that acts autonomously and hinders the consciousness and its natural flow.

00:09:55.168 --> 00:10:16.747
And he would let the people also repeat the words, and that would be the very cool thing. He would do the whole list, get the reaction words, get the time, and then he would repeat it. And the really interesting thing was when the people did the second run, the words with the longest reaction time, they could not remember. They get rid of everything.

00:10:17.358 --> 00:10:33.608
They, with a short reaction time, they would say the exact same word, but in the second run, long reaction time, they would say another word. So they could not remember what they have said. So his indication was there was something saying something in them that was something else.

00:10:35.294 --> 00:10:59.682
And it was Through this study set, he also came into contact with Freud because Freud developed at this time his theory of the unconscious and of complexes and so on. So Jung immediately was on board with Freud because he could prove what Freud was talking about and he could now explain what he could measure with his patients.

00:11:01.278 --> 00:11:15.767
And this was all developed also in his time in Burghölzli. And also the third book of the Collected Works called The Psychogenesis of Mental Disease happened there a lot.

00:11:15.815 --> 00:11:32.793
So let's say two-thirds, three-quarters of the book are again papers, especially about schizophrenia and psychosis and neuroses and hysteria and all this. So all those mental diseases that would prevent people from acting normally.

00:11:33.419 --> 00:11:58.050
And he would have a very different approach and thought process, which was new with psychoanalysis, to say people are still people even when they have psychological problems. The thought back then was when you have problems like schizophrenia or psychosis, your brain is damaged. That's a very materialistic view. So the brain is damaged, so you can't do anything.

00:11:58.226 --> 00:12:11.312
There would lock people up and it would be horrible conditions. And he would say, it's no wonder that all those people have psychological problems. Even normal people in these type of situations would develop problems very, very quickly.

00:12:12.039 --> 00:12:36.303
So the whole approach changed around this time to put people in facilities that are better served and that were clean. Where they would get good food and would be taken care of through this new psychoanalytic approach of the talking therapy. That means to take the person seriously, to let the person talk and try to understand the person.

00:12:37.121 --> 00:12:46.122
And he would write down a lot of his thoughts and approaches about schizophrenia and so on in different papers and publish it. And this is a collection.

00:12:46.603 --> 00:13:11.840
And the interesting thing here is, and I think this is makes this approach of the Collected Works better than Jung's original approach by having in this edition the topics clustered in single books, because here you have also his final thoughts close to the end of his life about schizophrenia and what it all meant and how it connected.

00:13:12.483 --> 00:13:20.360
So you get a history of his thoughts, how they originated, and how they were after 50, 60 years of practicing.

00:13:22.250 --> 00:13:43.844
And his psychoanalytic journey is well documented in the second book, uh, in the fourth, sorry, in the fourth book called Freud and Psychoanalysis, where it is a very interesting journey about the development of Jung in regards to psychoanalysis.

00:13:44.656 --> 00:14:05.857
As he starts, as I already told you before, very flaming for this topic because he thought, okay, there is something. It's some— finally someone found something that is of value and explains a lot. And we're still talking about Freud today. So Freud was very, very important for that time and very important for psychology and therapy and everything.

00:14:07.188 --> 00:14:18.480
And Jung always felt that in the beginning, And he has a lot of flaming letters defending Freud and psychoanalysis and everything. And this was before he met Freud.

00:14:18.980 --> 00:14:43.504
So when he met Freud, they became very close friends, worked together, they practiced together, they went together on journeys to America to give talks there and seminars. And in the book, Again, you have this journey of Jung, which means in the beginning he's completely for it.

00:14:44.210 --> 00:15:04.286
And with the progress of the book, his mind and approach changes, which means he becomes more critical. He points out the flaws. He says where Freud is wrong and where psychoanalysis is not enough. To explain certain things and phenomena.

00:15:05.234 --> 00:15:30.097
And you, so you already slowly feel away the drifting of Jung away from Freud. And this brings us to the end of this first big block of Jung's life. And the second block about the collective unconscious is brought in by this book. It's called Symbols of Transformation. Finally an English book.

00:15:31.769 --> 00:15:55.475
And this book was really the end of the friendship between Freud and Jung, as he would go into all the stuff that Freud didn't like. So he would go into mythology, religion, occultism, spirituality to have an alternative explanation for how the psyche works. And he sent this book.

00:15:55.718 --> 00:16:12.089
To Freud and Freud wrote him one letter back basically saying, okay, yeah, I see you're going now your own way and yeah, you can do what you want. But this practically ended the professional academic career of Jung.

00:16:13.116 --> 00:16:37.551
He stopped lecturing and he became a little bit like a pariah because he was this crowned prince of psychoanalysis. Was was very well regarded. He was supposed to bring psychoanalysis into a new generation. Freud was 20 years older than Jung. So Jung was the young guy who was supposed to take over and bring psychoanalysis forward.

00:16:38.904 --> 00:16:53.732
But he broke away. So also his, some friendships ended and his opportunities ended and he stopped teaching and had this let's say, midlife crisis.

00:16:53.780 --> 00:17:16.282
He was already in the late 30s and he did not really know how to go on because he always had this feeling that he only followed a system like psychoanalysis and he was a student and he was learning and so on. But he wanted to bring in more of himself and his interests and builds his own foundation.

00:17:16.717 --> 00:17:38.784
And Symbols of Transformation was a very, very important part of that. The original name was different. I think it was Psychology of the Unconscious or something like that. And only later he revised it in life completely. And this is a revised version that you get mostly today, where he streamlined it based on his later experiences.

00:17:42.743 --> 00:18:06.439
He then had a pause where he would, he didn't write so much because he had a very important experience. This was right before the First World War where he would get visions and have very hallucinatory experiences. And he was very afraid that he was going crazy and said, ah, it's ironic.

00:18:07.388 --> 00:18:30.073
I'm a psychotherapist and I now have schizophrenia. And this happened right before World War I, and there were visions about Europe and blood and a lot of corpses. And he thought he was going crazy until the war started. And he said, okay, yeah, I saw something. I experienced something. This is a little bit like a prophetic vision of the future and what will happen.

00:18:30.539 --> 00:18:47.535
It didn't help him in the situation back then, but as soon as the war started, He understood it. So he really went into his own unconscious to experience it and to document this. I will talk about this later more.

00:18:49.157 --> 00:19:22.041
I will continue now with the Collected Works, as his next book is a very famous book of his that's pointed to how Jung, the scientist, is well regarded in psychoanalysis because he wrote a very book over 8 years called Psychological Types, which is a way of describing types of people and how they interpret and experience the world.

00:19:22.686 --> 00:19:34.838
For example, extraversion and introversion are both words of Jung and concepts described by Jung. And he talks about further functions, thinking, feeling, sensation, intuition.

00:19:35.735 --> 00:19:54.997
And this book is very thick and also very well researched in the sense that he goes on for over 100 pages in the beginning, going through different personality models that existed before. He talks about Schiller. I think he talks about Aristotle.

00:19:55.687 --> 00:20:15.419
And he finds always those two types of persons that are described. They have these ones and these ones, right? And he uses this as a basis to bring his own explanation, to bring all those different personality models together, which is extraversion and introversion.

00:20:16.385 --> 00:20:36.024
And this is a very interesting approach, and he's been practicing at this time already psychoanalysis for a very long time and do therapy on people. So he used this as a basis to describe those different functions and combine them.

00:20:36.089 --> 00:20:58.127
So what, what is an introverted intuitive and an extraverted sensationist and so on? And very interesting book. And this is a singular book. So like the Collected Works, some books are singular books and some are collections. So Symbols of Transformation is a singular book.

00:20:58.950 --> 00:21:18.836
Psychological Types is a singular book. The other books I had before are rather collections of his works because they were often papers. So, but this he wrote after his Interesting episode of his unconscious talking to him.

00:21:20.581 --> 00:21:44.738
And there follows another very important book in regards to his psychology work. And this is very often used for training people for therapy and so on. And it's also a good introduction into Jungian psychology and his concepts infused in, in This is the book, is called Two Essays on Analytical Psychology.

00:21:46.490 --> 00:21:59.713
And this is really, let's say, more of a handbook. What therapy is about, how a therapist should be, how the relationship between the therapist and the patient should be.

00:22:00.710 --> 00:22:21.549
So it lays the groundwork, how the rules should be according to psychoanalysis or even Analytical Psychology, which was the Jungian twist on psychoanalysis. So psychoanalysis has this extreme focus on sex, and he shows a different approach.

00:22:21.613 --> 00:22:44.359
He said there are more forces in the psyche that are important and that push people outside of sex. This is a very good introduction to how his ideas and concepts are. And the next book, which is the 8th if you're counting, is The Structure and Dynamics of the Psyche.

00:22:45.811 --> 00:23:09.688
A big book again, and it's a collection. It's a collection of different essays and of different introductions he wrote and talks he gave, and they give a very good feeling about how this structure of the psyche that he's envisioning inside is working. So what parts are influencing each other?

00:23:10.474 --> 00:23:30.401
I really took out a lot of it because I did not read the books all in succession as here in the Collected Works, but rather I picked out singular books and then jumped a lot. And here he really goes into what he means with his libido concept. And this was the main thing I took out of it.

00:23:30.866 --> 00:23:55.355
How he thinks the psychic energy, what it is, what it does, how it manifests, how, where it goes into. So this is really useful, I think. And there are some books, when you have these singular books, that are pretty difficult to understand because C.G. Jung himself, I think, is not such a good writer.

00:23:56.078 --> 00:24:17.976
So when you have a book, he has chapters. Yeah, he structures this in some ways, but very often he jumps from concept to concept. So he mentions something in a side note and then moves on. He likes to let concepts interact, but he does not completely explain concepts. There are only single books.

00:24:18.482 --> 00:24:31.636
That really deal with this, or even chapters that deal what he means with a specific concept. And he has a lot of concepts and they interact in a lot of interesting ways, but they are distributed very finely throughout the Collected Works.

00:24:31.700 --> 00:24:51.919
So I can only understand certain things because I read everything, because you pick up a letter here, you pick up a seminar here, there you have a book introduction, and you just have these very simple sentences that go into, oh yeah, and this is this and this. All right. Yeah, of course. And just by collecting this, you suddenly start to understand it.

00:24:52.642 --> 00:25:16.760
So this is pretty good because it's rather short essays and introductions and so on, and talks he gave, which— better, I think he was a better talker than he was a writer. But I guess it was also very difficult to write about what he's been writing about because it's such complex topics. In here is also the book Synchronicity.

00:25:16.857 --> 00:25:40.985
And this is also a very difficult short book, but it gets a lot of attention, especially when people are interested in astrology or spiritual stuff. They're often very familiar with synchronicity. Yeah, that would be for that book. And We are still in the second block of his life.

00:25:41.050 --> 00:26:03.762
We're still in the collective unconscious. And he has this book, The Archetypes and the Collective Unconscious, which is a pretty good book. So you get what's in the package. It's very direct. You get everything I talked about the last event about what are archetypes, how they work, how they manifest, how they can be experienced.

00:26:04.035 --> 00:26:19.982
So it's very spot on, and he's also talking a lot about individuation, which is his idea of how a person develops as a person in their lives and how the psyche changes over the lifespan.

00:26:21.550 --> 00:26:44.319
So this is already a good introduction, which has good chapter pretty close to the end where he has a female patient. Who has trouble in her life. She's already in her 40s and she's very smart, but she does not know really what to do. And one thing that Jung very often did with his patients, especially in this point in life, he let them draw.

00:26:46.232 --> 00:27:04.285
And very often these people were not artistic in any way. They have never done something artistic. He said, it's not important that you draw well, but rather draw where you have the feeling what you want to draw. So people would start and then get more and more ideas, and these pictures would have a very special value for them.

00:27:05.086 --> 00:27:29.117
And he followed her and had her as a patient for many years. And in the back, there's a collection of all her paintings and how they developed. So he shows these paintings and explains on them what happens in her psyche and how it was changing through individuation, through exploration. Of the unconscious. So very good book.

00:27:29.826 --> 00:27:53.967
The next one is one of the most famous ones, which is Aion, which is an incredibly difficult book. So the first chapter is great. It's amazing when you want to have introduction into Jung. The first chapter explains shadow, anima, animus, Self. It's very straightforward. This book was written pretty late in life. He published it in '76.

00:27:54.616 --> 00:28:15.100
I think he worked on it for 10 years or so. It's really this exploration, as it even says on the cover, Researches into the Phenomenology of the Self. And he goes further than, let's say, this second part of his block.

00:28:16.400 --> 00:28:37.616
So this second block of the collective unconscious and pushes into all the alchemy stuff. So while the first chapter is great and you can follow it pretty well, after it starts talking about these aeons, which are, let's say, time periods where the collective unconscious changes.

00:28:38.436 --> 00:29:03.078
So not only the individual psyche changes through time, but also the collective. And he saw that as represented in astrology and the stars as a representation of that. So not the stars influence the psyche, but rather there's something in the psyche that projects itself onto the stars. And so he's talking a lot about how Jesus is a fish.

00:29:04.410 --> 00:29:27.243
This is almost the whole book where he tries to replicate what happened or explain what happened over 2,000 years ago and how the mentality shifted based off this shift of the Aion into fishes and what this meant psychologically.

00:29:27.728 --> 00:29:49.255
So it's difficult to get into because it's a little bit hard to do, get the concepts. And also there are already some alchemy stuff. That makes it difficult. So Alchemy, it's a third block and the big last block of his life, and you need to understand practically everything that's happened before to get into Alchemy. That's my recommendation.

00:29:51.580 --> 00:30:16.044
The next book is Civilization in Transition, which I thought was great. It's another collection similar as The dynamics, structure and dynamics of the unconscious. And here something is a little bit weird. Normally the English books are rather cheap when you want to get it from Jung, except, except when you want to get these collected works.

00:30:16.093 --> 00:30:36.472
So when you have something that's famous like Aion or The Structure and Dynamics of the Psyche, those are famous, those are cheap. But when you want to get those collected works in English, they're very expensive. That cost like €80, €90. In Germany, they cost €20 or €25 tops, which is a little bit strange because I really like them because they're collections.

00:30:36.953 --> 00:30:55.351
You have shorter essays, shorter talks, some seminars, and they are very, very good to at least talk about one concept only. When he gives a talk, he mostly talks about one concept when it's a short talk or when he has a book introduction. Is mostly about one concept.

00:30:55.720 --> 00:31:19.034
That makes it easy to get into these concepts because you now have finally a short introduction, something that is missing a little bit from the overall Collected Works, that you don't really have this dictionary except for— and this I didn't talk about— in Psychological Types, you have in the back a short dictionary where he talks about all the terms that he's using in the book with a short description.

00:31:19.227 --> 00:31:39.230
And this is great. This is amazing. So when you read Or should you read Psychological Types? Be aware that's in the back. It's a dictionary that can also help you out to quickly get into terms and understand what he's meaning. So you have a lot of amazing short books in here. You have Modern Man in Search of a Soul, which also gets recommended always very highly.

00:31:39.696 --> 00:32:01.674
You have Earth and Soul, which I really liked and thought it was a great introduction, but I never hear it recommended anywhere. And you have a book about UFOs. UFOs. He investigated this because he was really interested in everything which is a product of the psyche and product of culture.

00:32:02.205 --> 00:32:14.000
So he went into it and tried to understand the psychological reason for UFOs, which is just a fun exercise to experience.

00:32:14.000 --> 00:32:38.037
One of his ideas was that Earth is getting overcrowded, so the people are looking into the stars as a way to escape the overcrowded Earth, which is kind of funny because he had 5 children and in his lifetime a minimum of 19 grandchildren. So, and he's concerned about overcrowding.

00:32:38.662 --> 00:32:54.797
On the Earth, which is a little bit hypocritical. So like, okay, what did you contribute that the Earth does not overcrowd? But it was a valid concern back then because medicine got better, nutrition got better, people had a lot of children, and suddenly they all survived. So a lot of changes.

00:32:56.066 --> 00:33:18.646
The next book is— oh wait, I want to say something again because this— I really like this one. When you have the chance, Civilization in Transition. Because he tries to connect it to modernity. He talks about a lot National Socialism and what he thinks happened there because he saw it live. He experienced World War I live. He experienced World War II live.

00:33:19.520 --> 00:33:41.520
And he says that he saw already years before the Second World War certain patterns in the dreams of Germans that concerned him a little bit. And that started to happen more and more. So this is interesting. It's called Wotan, like the Nordic god, and highly recommend this book.

00:33:43.314 --> 00:33:55.914
The next book is a collection again, and it's about the psychology of East and West. He was very interested in religion because religion is very old. It's full of symbols. It's full of stories.

00:33:57.037 --> 00:34:21.363
And it gives indication how the psyche works because he sees culture especially as something that comes from the unconscious, the collective unconscious, and helps people to navigate these psychological perils that can come up. So it's about the West and the East. He contrasts those a lot. As he said, they are both a side of the same coin. So they work differently.

00:34:22.264 --> 00:34:44.908
But they are connected in a way. What a lot of people who are interested in Jung are not aware about is he was really interested in Christian religion and he talks about it a lot, really a lot. And he's a fan of Catholicism in the way that he says that it works in a therapeutic way.

00:34:46.590 --> 00:35:09.726
He saw that And this was his idea that the need for psychology came up only with the downfall of organized religions in Europe. As he said before, religions in all ages had a way of supporting people psychologically, but when that fell away, people were suddenly alone with their psychology. So, and their unconscious.

00:35:09.951 --> 00:35:29.313
So the experience of the unconscious only came up. When suddenly it became an issue. And he said the consciousness of civilization changed in a way that these old symbols of religion have lost their value and they have to be now rediscovered in a different frame of mind.

00:35:29.361 --> 00:35:51.847
This goes again into Aions, where just the basic way how the psyche functions is changing. And so this is psychology, as the youngest science, as he said, is to fill this. And he explains this a lot in this book where he, I think, rather talks more about Western religion than Eastern religion.

00:35:52.235 --> 00:36:16.940
But it's very interesting to get this contrast of him consciously. And he's especially, yeah, not a fan of importing Eastern religion and symbols into Europe because he says it's too far away from the cultural understanding. Maybe it's different today, but back then this was all very new and like fancy and people didn't know a lot about it.

00:36:17.455 --> 00:36:38.225
So they started to project all this other stuff they had inside of themselves into it, which was not really there. I think it's also a great way to start. As he talks about A lot of that stuff that's familiar, that people have knowledge about.

00:36:38.968 --> 00:37:03.201
And one book, especially in here, is very, very interesting and famous. It's Answer to Job. It's, let's say, a psychoanalytic explanation for this story of Job and how he got punished by God. What this tells about God. He was very interested into the God-image.

00:37:03.313 --> 00:37:23.341
He said he can't talk about God himself, that would be metaphysics, but he can talk about how humanity has talked about God. And this is very interesting book to basically give God a psychology and try to explain it in context of this story. Pretty famous.

00:37:23.469 --> 00:37:43.580
